{"causes": ["The decline of the Khmer Empire's direct control over regional polities allowed local leaders like Sri Indraditya to establish independent kingdoms.", "The establishment of the Phra Ruang dynasty by his father, Sri Indraditya, created the political framework for Ban Mueang's succession.", "Sukhothai's strategic location in the Chao Phraya basin fostered its emergence as a significant regional power center."], "effects": ["Ban Mueang's reign ensured the continuity of the Phra Ruang dynasty's rule in Sukhothai, stabilizing the nascent kingdom.", "His death in 1279 directly led to the ascension of his younger brother, Ram Khamhaeng, who greatly expanded Sukhothai's power.", "The stability maintained during his rule provided a secure foundation for Sukhothai's subsequent cultural and territorial expansion."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "sri-indraditya", "sourceName": "Sri Indraditya", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "ban-mueang", "targetName": "Ban Mueang", "context": "Sri Indraditya's establishment of the Sukhothai Kingdom and the Phra Ruang dynasty directly enabled Ban Mueang's succession to the throne."}, {"sourceSlug": "ban-mueang", "sourceName": "Ban Mueang", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "medieval-thailand", "targetName": "Medieval Thailand", "context": "Ban Mueang's reign as king of Sukhothai occurred within the broader historical period of medieval Southeast Asia, specifically in what is now Thailand."}, {"sourceSlug": "ban-mueang", "sourceName": "Ban Mueang", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "sri-indraditya", "targetName": "Sri Indraditya", "context": "Ban Mueang succeeded his father, Sri Indraditya, as the second king of the Sukhothai Kingdom around the late 13th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "ram-khamhaeng", "sourceName": "Ram Khamhaeng", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "ban-mueang", "targetName": "Ban Mueang", "context": "Ram Khamhaeng succeeded his elder brother, Ban Mueang, as king of Sukhothai upon the latter's death in 1279, initiating a period of significant expansion."}, {"sourceSlug": "ban-mueang", "sourceName": "Ban Mueang", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "sukhothai-kingdom", "targetName": "Sukhothai Kingdom", "context": "Ban Mueang served as a monarch of the Sukhothai Kingdom, the foundational state of the Phra Ruang dynasty in 13th-century Thailand."}], "places": [{"name": "Sukhothai, Thailand", "role": "Capital City"}, {"name": "Central Plains, Thailand", "role": "Region of Rule"}, {"name": "Southeast Asia", "role": "Geopolitical Context"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Ban Mueang", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "ban-mueang"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2881860", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ban_Mueang"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Ban Mueang's reign was crucial for ensuring the stable succession and continuity of the nascent Sukhothai Kingdom, directly enabling the later, more expansive rule of his brother Ram Khamhaeng the Great. His brief but steady leadership prevented potential power vacuums and solidified the Phra Ruang dynasty's early authority.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-13T07:33:49Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27460354357", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Ban Mueang was a king of Sukhothai, an ancient kingdom in Thailand. He was from the Phra Ruang dynasty.", "newValue": "Ban Mueang, a monarch of the nascent Sukhothai Kingdom, presided over a pivotal, albeit brief, transitional period in Southeast Asian history, ending his reign with his death in 1279. As a ruler of the Phra Ruang dynasty, which laid foundational cultural and political structures for what would become Thailand, Ban Mueang's tenure followed his father, Sri Indraditya, the kingdom's founder, and immediately preceded the transformative reign of his younger brother, Ram Khamhaeng the Great. His rule ensured the stability of the nascent state, bridging the gap between its establishment and its subsequent expansion into a regional power.

While precise details of Ban Mueang's administration are scarce, historical chronicles and epigraphic evidence, particularly the Ram Khamhaeng Inscription (Stone Inscription No. 1), suggest he played a crucial role in consolidating the early Sukhothai state. His reign likely focused on maintaining the territorial integrity established by his father and fostering the internal cohesion of the kingdom, which had only recently asserted its independence from Khmer influence. He is believed to have engaged in local skirmishes or diplomatic efforts to secure Sukhothai's borders and ensure the succession within the Phra Ruang lineage.

